WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.840
[EXPLOSION]

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:07.880
My God.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.960
If you breathe a word of
what you've seen here,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.120
we'll do time for treason.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.760
 I hate this!

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.040
I know you do.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.960
Do you have any idea
what a pain you are?

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:20.720
I think so, sir.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.320
Jim, the beach is that way.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.360
You all right?

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.840
You dare threaten me?

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.200
Thor, so puny--

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:40.840
What?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:41.840
He was freaking me out.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.240
Hey.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.480
This is the first marker.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.560
The path was a lie.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.920
You're talking about
the moment of creation.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.080
Open the doors and tell
the world the truth.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.520
I'm Tony Stark.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.760
I build neat stuff.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.280
I got a great girl.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.400
And occasionally,
I save the world.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.120
So why can't I sleep?

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.520
Two more.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.120
Girls waiting over here.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.560
I got you now.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.440
I got everything going for me.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.320
Life is funny.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.600
You girls.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.360
It's all so strange
how things can

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.240
be so different than you think.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.320
You know what I
always wanted to be?

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.960
President of the United States.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.760
Look when you're in office,
you got to do a lot of things,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.200
sometimes, that are not
always in the strictest sense

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.520
of the law legal.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.880
But you do them because
they're in the greater

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.000
interests of the nation.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.520
Wait, just so I
understand correctly.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.320
Are you really saying
that in certain situations

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.440
the president can decide
whether it's in the best

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.440
interests of the nation.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.640
And then, do something illegal--

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.440
I'm saying that when
the president does it,

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.480
that means it's not illegal.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.440
Hi, everyone.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.560
And welcome to the Academy's
Visiting Artists Series.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.960
The film made
addition for sound.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.840
My name is Teri Dorman.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.520
I am a supervising
dialogue editor.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.760
I also am one of the three
governors in the sound branch

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.720
of the Academy.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.000
And it is--

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.400
I am really happy to be
joined by Peter Devlin

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.080
today, who is a
production mixer.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.640
At the top of this, we saw
a few samples of his work.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.680
Peter has been a production
sound mixer for over 30 years.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.600
He's been nominated for an
Academy Award five times.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.400
He has worked on over 75 films.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.920
And it is my
pleasure to introduce

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.760
my friend Peter Devlin.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:58.680
Hi, Peter.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.360
Hi Teri, thank you very
much for that introduction.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.320
And I should note
that there's some

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.960
of your work in those clips
as well, since our association

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.200
goes back many years.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.880
Yes, I was lucky to have
worked on your tracks.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.480
We've done, I think, seven films
together during that 22 year

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.200
period, I think.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.760
But why don't we start
from the beginning, Peter.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.040
Could you tell me a little
bit about your history?

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.920
How you got started
in the industry?

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.400
And what things might have
inspired you to go into sound?

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.680
Sure, well, I'm originally from
Belfast in Northern Ireland.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.040
And in the '70s,
Northern Ireland

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.480
was going through
a turbulent time.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.960
And I can remember
just the importance

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.320
of going to the movies.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.800
And I just absolutely
loved going to the movies.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.440
We lived in Belfast
for a period of time.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.520
Then, we moved to a
little coastal town

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.520
called Port Stuart.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.320
So it was quite difficult
to get to the movies.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.280
But there was one movie that
I definitely wanted to see,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.840
which was in the summer of '76--

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.040
'76 or '77, and it was Jaws.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.200
Jaws was the film
that totally changed

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.920
my life and my direction.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.520
And it was a film that
obviously got a lot of hype

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.440
here in the US.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.040
But it was, I
think, a book that I

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.000
discovered called The Jaws Log
by the writer Carl Gottlieb.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.800
And he worked on
the film as well.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.720
But that book-- and
I'll just grab it here.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.720
I actually still have
the book, The Jaws Log.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.480
This was a behind the scenes
of the making the film.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:39.880
And that just--

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.200
I couldn't wait to see
the film because it just

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.160
seemed an eternity from
when I was reading about how

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.200
the film was made to
actually seeing it

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.160
in the theater in Belfast.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.920
And in the theater in Belfast,
you call it the cinema.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:53.200
We went to the cinema.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.080
And I went with my cousin who
was two years older than me.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.600
And I remember just sitting
with such excitement.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.680
And those opening
chords of John Williams.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.440
And what was your
first job in sound?

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.720
Well, the thing is, my
father was an accountant.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.960
The rest of my
family were teachers.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.120
I went a very
non-traditional route

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.040
in that I was encouraged to
take up civil engineering

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.680
at the local university.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.440
And what I wanted to
do was get into film.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.720
And I have no idea how to do it.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.600
My careers office
here at school wasn't

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.680
able to point me in
the right direction.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.320
And my principal
of my school when

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.360
I was leaving in the
summer of '79, 1980,

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.360
he said to me this idea of
working in the film business

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.240
or television business
was pie in the sky.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.080
It always stayed with me,
that line pie in the sky.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.560
And I'm like, well, I'm going
to figure out how to do this.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.120
And fortunately, there
was an advertisement

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.440
in the local newspaper.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.560
And it was for a
trainee audio assistant.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:57.000
And I thought, well,
the BBC produces

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.880
radio programs, television
dramas, that's a great way.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.640
And of course, I knew
nothing really about audio.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.800
I was fascinated with
film-- the language of film,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.200
but knew nothing about audio.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.440
So I remember I sent in the
application and thought,

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.440
well, if I get called
for an interview-- if I'm

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.920
lucky to be called
for an interview,

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.200
I need to do some research.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.000
And I went into
the local library

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.320
and found books
and sound recording

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.840
and basically crammed this
information-- took notes down.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.360
And I got a letter from the BBC
inviting me for an interview.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:32.080
And I remember going for that
interview and it was just--

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.240
it was, I think,
a panel of four.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.560
And they talked to me about what
is it I liked about the BBC.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.960
What is it I liked about radio,
television, did I enjoy films?

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.560
And they were able to focus me
on just my general interests.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.600
But then, they started to
get into the hard stuff

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:51.560
of sound recording.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.200
And fortunately, from
my visit to the library,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.960
I had enough experience to
skid through that interview.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.560
And then, I did a
second interview

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.960
with the head of engineering and
it seemed obviously past that.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.880
And then, got invited
to join the BBC.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.040
So I had to go on a
training course, which

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.120
started, actually, it's 40 years
ago this month in April of '81

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.880
is when I set off from Belfast
to the BBC'S Engineering Center

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.200
just outside Birmingham.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:25.720
A little town called Evesham.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.600
And it was an
amazing facility that

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.640
had people that were working
in film editing, that

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.880
were working in directing
and camera and sound.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.080
So the BBC gave me
this amazing training

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.840
and all sorts of disciplines.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.000
So when I did my training
course, I went back to Belfast

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.960
and I worked in news, I
worked in current affairs,

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.640
I worked in radio dramas.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.960
And I found myself leaning
towards the drama element.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.000
I loved being in a
radio studio being

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.320
a participant in a radio drama.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.880
Doing sound effects-- Foley.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.680
So anyway, that's
the short version.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.040
It's interesting
that your experience

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.960
is practical
experience as opposed

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.240
to going in film school.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.520
But what got you from
Ireland to the United States?

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:13.640
Well, there's an
interesting journey.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:15.760
So I'm working at
the BBC in Belfast

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.600
and I'm thinking,
OK, what I really

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.640
want to do is work in movies.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:24.040
I'm going to cinemas, I'm
watching Apocalypse Now,

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.080
I'm watching Star Trek.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.440
I'm really enjoying big
blockbusters from the US.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:33.000
And I started to
write to studios.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.640
I wrote to 20th Century Fox.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.960
I wrote to Warner Brothers.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.400
I wrote, I think, to the
producers of Steven Spielberg's

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.280
Indiana Jones to say, dear sir
or madam, I work for the BBC

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:44.880
in Belfast.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.000
Could I visit the set?

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.320
Of course, I didn't get
very many responses.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:53.400
But I got a response
from one filmmaker

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.400
that I admire hugely and
his name is Michael Mann.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.480
I wrote to Michael Mann in
'87 and said, Dear Mr. Mann,

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.080
I'm a sound engineer working
for the BBC in Belfast.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.840
I'm a huge fan of your
television series Miami Vice

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.320
because Miami Vice was one
of the more popular TV shows

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.640
in the UK in-- from 1985 to 87.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.400
And I asked him, would it be
possible to visit the set?

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.320
Because I was really
intrigued with just--

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.640
it was a high--

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.240
it was a very glossy production,
big budget car chases,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.640
and the music was
such an important part

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:30.880
of the sound of it.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.360
So I sent this letter
off and it was within,

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.440
I think, a month
I got a response.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.720
And I just remember it came
into the crew room in BBC

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:40.680
in Belfast.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.200
And I saw the Miami
Vice logo and my heart

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.320
was beating so fast.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:48.160
And I thought, OK, it's
going to be a rejection.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.920
Dear sir, thank you
for your interest

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.960
but here was Michael Mann--
sound from Michael Mann

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.800
inviting me to the
set of Miami Vice.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.960
So that letter changed
the course of my life.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.280
So never underestimate the
power of the written letter as

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:03.880
opposed to the written email.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.440
A written letter really
speaks for something.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:10.680
So that's the first step
in my journey to the US.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.400
It's also a good
lesson to anybody

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.800
wanting to get in the industry
is putting yourself out there.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:18.280
And you never know.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.880
All you've got to do is
ask at a certain point.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:21.640
Persistence.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:25.720
I mean, Chris Monroe, who's
an acclaimed production

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.320
mixer from the UK
talks about how

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.600
he was working at a gas petrol
station in the UK when he--

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.160
somebody pulled in in a car.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.520
And he heard something about he
worked at the film studio at--

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.080
I'm not sure whether it
was Pinewood or Elstree.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.040
But just from hearing--

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.920
overhearing that conversation,
Chris spoke to this guy.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.880
And the next thing is that kind
of opened up the door for him

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:51.920
into the film industry.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.160
Everybody has a way of
managing it, I think,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.720
if they can follow their dreams.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.880
Always to have that
focus and persistence.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.520
Absolutely, I
think that's great.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.840
And your first job
in the United States.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:06.120
You moved to Florida.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:06.800
Is that correct?

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:07.920
I moved to Florida.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.600
And that really was a result
of that letter to Michael Mann,

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:11.080
again.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:14.760
When I visited the set
of Miami Vice, I spent,

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.080
I think, it was two days
with the production team.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:20.320
And I remember when I arrived
on set the assistant directors

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:23.640
were telling me, don't
talk to the actors.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.600
And I'm like, I'm not
interested in the actors.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.400
I want to hang out
with the sound team.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.600
And the production mixer was
a gentleman called Joe Foglia.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:36.440
And Joe was mixing the TV series
with a boom operator Scott

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:37.600
Blander--

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.040
sorry, Jeff Blander
and Vince Nuccio.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:41.880
So that was a three person team.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:48.720
And Joe contacted me when I went
back to Belfast after the fact

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.720
and said, hey, look, you seem
quite interested in working

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:52.440
in the US.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.560
When I'm doing Miami
Vice, my company

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.760
does commercials,
low budget indies,

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:02.080
would you be interested
in joining the company?

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.800
So I went to my boss
at the BBC and say,

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.600
I've been given this
opportunity in the US.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:10.160
Could I go out there
and see what it's like?

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.320
Could you keep my job open
here at the BBC in Belfast?

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.680
And he said, well, that sounds
like a great opportunity,

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.760
but we definitely would not
keep your job open here.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.360
You got to go for it.

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:24.720
So I left in Christmas of
'87 having been with the BBC

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:26.280
for almost seven years.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.360
And thought, OK,
I'll give it a go.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.200
And I went to Miami
and that's where

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.000
I started my career in the US.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:37.720
And after that, were you working
on many feature films in--

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:41.200
No, I started in a
television series,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.280
which is called The
Adventures of Superboy.

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.200
It's my kind of first
foray into superheroes,

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.880
which I continue to
this day having just

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:52.360
worked on Wonder Woman
84 that just came out

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:56.640
recently and in the world
of robots with Transformers

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.040
and Iron Man.

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:01.320
So it all started with The
Adventures of Superboy,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.120
which was produced by the
same team behind the Superman

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.440
movies.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.560
They produced a movie called
Christopher Columbus Discovery.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.320
So that was kind of the
first big feature film I did,

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:12.720
which was in 1991.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.920
And that was with Marlon Brando.

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:19.760
That was kind of early
days in this sound career.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.040
And in, may I say,
just technically

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:25.480
to that was in the days
of 1/4 inch recording.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:26.480
Recording on tape.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.760
And we've come a long way
since those 1/4 inch days.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.520
I think we can get into the
technology changes a little bit

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.000
later.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.720
Because that certainly affected
your career and my career.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.000
But when you first started, did
you start as a production mixer

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.920
or did you start in an assistant
capacity as a boom operator?

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.280
No, I started as an assistant.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:52.200
I mean, the wonderful thing
about working from the BBC

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:55.800
is one week I could be working
on the Elstra orchestra

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.400
setting microphones,
the next week I

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:01.560
could be working one of
the current affair shows,

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:06.280
running the board, which was, I
think, like a 16 channel board

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:08.760
with outside sources
of telephones.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:14.480
And if you were doing a live
link to a reporter in London

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:16.440
or in Wales or Scotland.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:22.600
So working in-- at the BBC
give me that opportunity just

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:25.520
to look at all the
different areas of sound,

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:32.440
whether it be working at a
boxing match or a soccer match,

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:37.120
rugby, a musical show,
traditional music--

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.920
traditional Irish show.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:39.800
There's a TV series

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:40.840
called, "As I Roved Out".

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:41.720
.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:45.160
And each week, they
had a different band

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:45.960
that would come in.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.240
Sometimes it was Van
Morrison and the Chieftains.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:51.520
So I would have been working
in the floor as an assistant.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:55.720
John Lund was one of our senior
recording engineers at the BBC

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.120
who was absolutely
amazing on the panel.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:01.480
So I was lucky to have
great mentors at the BBC.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:05.960
Dixie Dean, who was
head of the drama.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.000
He was the person that
would always end up

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.000
mixing on the drama show.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.520
So Dixie was somebody that
took me under his wing

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:20.640
and allowed me to do second
boom on the fisher boom, which

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.600
is the platform boom on dramas.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.880
And then, there was another
mixer called Peter Lindsay

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:27.920
that worked in film dramas.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.280
I worked with him.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:33.000
So I had a little bit of time
in all the different disciplines

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:33.520
of sound.

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:36.640
And that's what was so great
about working for the BBC.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:39.880
Being able to work in
those assistant capacities.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:42.080
And also stepping up to mixing.

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:43.680
When I left the BBC, it was--

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.280
I was an audio supervisor.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:49.600
So I was starting
to do bigger shows.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:53.200
But my love was really I wanted
to get into feature films.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:57.280
It sounds like your experience
there would benefit you

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.920
later in some of the
films that you took on

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:01.480
because you were
working with crowds,

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:05.520
you were working with
various situations that

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.520
would enable you to do
films like Any Given

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:11.280
Sunday where you were
dealing with large crowds.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.960
Yeah, you bring up
Any Given Sunday.

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:19.240
And I think that was a
particularly difficult film

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:21.800
because it was--

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.440
technically, it
was just at a stage

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:25.480
where we were
still transitioning

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:28.080
from digital audio
tape to what's

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.000
going to happen next
as a recording medium.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.600
And it was where I
would have loved to have

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.600
had a lot more channels to--

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.400
because there was so much
happening on the different

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:41.640
sets that we were filming on.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:46.760
But there's a scene in the
film that so many people

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:47.720
have talked about.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:51.920
And it was where Al
Pacino as Tony D'amato

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:55.480
was giving a pep talk to the--

00:16:55.480 --> 00:17:00.560
the teammates-- his team
in the locker rooms.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:04.280
Because that's what living is.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:07.520
The six inches in
front of your face.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:10.320
Now, I can't make you do it.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.280
You've got to look
at the guy to you.

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.200
Look into his eyes.

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:17.000
Now, I think you're
going to see a guy who

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.120
will go that inch with you.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:23.080
You're going to see a guy
who will sacrifice himself

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:27.240
for this team because he knows
when it comes down to it,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:30.920
you're going to do
the same for him.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:33.600
That's a team, gentlemen.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:40.160
And either we heal
now as a team,

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:43.440
or we will die as individuals.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:48.880
That's football, guys.

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:54.040
That's all it is.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:59.480
Now, what are you going to do?

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:08.280
[SHOUTING]

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:13.120
It's such an
inspirational speech

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.840
that I just-- every
time I see it,

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:17.240
it stops me because
I get goose bumps.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:19.600
Because I remember
that day and I remember

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:22.080
Pacino was just so perfect.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:24.920
Somehow or other, you
compensated because what's

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:27.200
on the screen is quite amazing.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:29.840
If you look at that
film and analyze

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:32.240
how many things are
going on at once,

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:35.480
and you're coverage of
that is pretty phenomenal.

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:39.080
As a sound mixer, it's
all about the authenticity

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:39.880
of performance.

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:42.160
And even looking at
those opening clips

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.520
that I was looking
at this morning,

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:47.840
whether it be Chris Pine
as Captain Kirk or--

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:54.520
and Charlize Theron as
Aileen Wuornos in Monster.

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:59.240
Our job as sound mixers is
to record a true performance.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:04.200
And I'm always amazed at
how between action and cut

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:07.120
that they create this magic.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.960
And the role of a
production mixer

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:12.680
is so different to what
happens in post-production.

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:17.280
We're just trying to fight
all the different elements.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.640
I think this is a
good point to make

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:23.120
at this point in
our conversation

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:26.040
is that the different
levels of sound

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:28.800
and the fact that
you're at the beginning

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:33.080
and what you are
recording and capturing

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:38.640
is the true performances of
the actors, the actual what's

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:41.760
going on on the set, whether
or not it's a practical set

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:43.680
or whether it's a sound stage.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:47.400
And your position on
this is the beginning

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:50.800
of that circle of talent,
specifically dealing

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.680
with your production mixing.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:57.840
I'd like to know who your team
of people is that back you up.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:02.360
On any given project, how
many people are with you?

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:06.280
Well, traditionally,
you have yourself

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:10.320
as the production mixer and
you have your boom operator

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:11.680
and you have a utility.

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:14.520
And that utility is the--

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:17.080
I was going to say least
experienced, person

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:19.080
but you really need
an experienced person.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:22.280
Because the utility really is
a second boom operator as well.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:25.800
And he or she may not be as
experienced as the main boom

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:26.640
operator.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:30.040
But they have to come in,
normally, at the last minute

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:31.600
to be the additional boom.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:35.920
Now, because of the way
films have become bigger,

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:37.520
there's more cameras.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.400
There are more-- there
are bigger sound crews now

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:41.520
in the UK.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:45.920
You would find Simon Hayes when
he was doing Les Miserables

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:50.120
or Chris Monroe who's on
the new Mission Impossible

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:51.960
film is working with--

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:55.280
their term as assistant sounds
to first assistant sound

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.240
and a second assistant sound.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:01.440
And they're sometimes trainees
on those films as well.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:05.680
So you've normally got a three
or a four person sound crew.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.520
On Any Given Sunday,
it was much bigger

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:10.040
because we were able
to have a second unit.

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:13.720
But the boom operator and the
second boom operator or utility

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:16.600
are basically your eyes
and ears on the set.

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:18.240
They're your voice on the set.

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:22.840
And I worked with Kevin
Cerchiai for many years

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:25.000
who's originally from Miami.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:27.160
He now works in Atlanta.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:29.400
And there's a
shorthand between you.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:32.200
I'm sure you find yourself--
when you're in the cutting room

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.480
if you're working
with another colleague

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:36.080
and you've worked
with them for years,

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.240
there is a shorthand
between the two of you.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.720
You know exactly how
each other works.

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:43.960
So you and I have both
worked on Michael Bay films.

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:46.880
And Michael's films
are big, spectacular.

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:49.760
Pearl Harbor was a
tough film because

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:51.000
of the scale of the film.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.280
But also the fact
that Michael likes

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:56.040
to use multiple
cameras and also likes

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.840
to use a lot of
pyrotechnics effects.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.520
And you're trying to get
that dialogue in there.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:05.040
And if there's a film that
changed the course of my life,

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:06.960
it was working with
him on Bad Boys.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:10.200
I need backup on Canal.

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:11.320
Now.

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:12.960
[GUNSHOTS]

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:18.120
Just get ready to shoot.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:21.520
[ENGINES REVVING]

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:23.320
Police.

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:26.680
Stop the car.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:28.120
Don't show me your badge.

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:30.120
He has a gun, shoot him.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:32.480
[GUNSHOTS]

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:38.480
His films are big and bold.

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:39.920
The sound design is big.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:44.080
And sometimes, it's
a wrestling match

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:47.480
to save that production
track, as you well know.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:50.120
Yes, I was going
to say with the--

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:52.560
the thing is you've
now worked with Michael

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:54.200
Bay on several films.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:57.320
Does that give you a
shorthand with the director

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:01.200
so that you possibly have his
ear so you can communicate

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:04.760
to him whether or not the
sound in that particular area--

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:08.480
I know Michael of all
people is very concerned

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.520
about his visual shot of things.

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:15.920
I remember in Pearl Harbor there
was a scene in the hospital.

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:19.640
And the curtains were blowing.

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:24.680
And it was the wind machines
that were blowing the curtains.

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:27.360
And he didn't care
about the dialogue

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:28.840
at that particular
point, he wanted

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:32.280
to make sure that those
curtains were blowing.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.840
First of all, when it comes to
the actors within that scene,

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.760
you want to make sure
they're all radio miked.

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.480
There used to be a
time where you would--

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:42.800
when a director comes
in to block a scene,

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:45.440
you would try to say,
OK, we can boom this.

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:47.120
We can maybe use
a radiomic there.

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:49.400
But now, it's
traditional to kind

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:52.080
of put radiomics on the actors
because you don't have time

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.600
to make that change.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:58.600
And back in 2000 when we
were shooting Pearl Harbor,

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:02.800
I was using a Diva 2, which
is a four track recorder.

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:05.960
And I remember when Michael
blocked that scene, I'm like,

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:07.280
I don't have enough channels.

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:11.480
I don't have enough
channels on this recorder

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.000
to be able to do this scene.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:15.000
And then, I think I
pulled in probably a DAT

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:19.880
machine, which was able to give
me an additional two tracks.

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:22.560
And then, you start
to prioritize, OK,

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:26.960
so we can use a boom mic
on this part of the scene.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:28.360
We can use a radio here.

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:32.240
So it's really just
try to isolate and look

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:34.320
at the priorities
within that scene.

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:38.160
And today, behind me
here, this recorder

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:39.760
is able to do 24 tracks.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.480
I would love to have
had that back then.

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:45.200
But you do what you
can with the tools

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:48.400
that you've got not knowing
what lies ahead in the future.

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:51.800
But that particular
scene, because

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:56.040
of the movement of,
basically, the boom operator

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:57.920
Donovan Dear moved
with the camera.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:24:59.960
So it was almost
documentary style.

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:03.680
Almost bringing what I learned
from my background and working

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:06.560
at the BBC and just what
one microphone can do.

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:08.200
And I'll give you an example.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.400
I have a little microphone here.

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:15.560
One microphone can do so
much to capture a scene.

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:19.080
And there are times
where you can just

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:23.120
get focused in putting too
many radiomics out there.

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:27.120
So but one microphone
can do so much.

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:31.640
And probably, a lot of that was
done on one microphone as well.

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:36.480
And we like to use stereo mics
as well for crowd sequences.

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:39.320
I think people
also need to know,

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:41.800
you're not actually
right there where

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:44.800
the action is taking place.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.320
You're set aside.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:49.520
It's kind of hard to describe
your physical distance

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:52.640
from where the
actors are performing

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:54.000
that particular scene.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:57.560
It's mostly-- you touched on
it to say that it's your boom

00:25:57.560 --> 00:25:59.520
operator that's right there.

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:02.720
So I think it would
be helpful to show

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:07.320
your distance from the
actual scene being shot.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:09.680
Yeah, I mean, on
Any Given Sunday,

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:11.840
I was quite far from the action.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.080
And we would have to
run a multi-cable out

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:18.560
to our wireless receivers
to get the signal back.

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:20.400
So every project is different.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:23.920
And now, with the reality
of the COVID world,

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:28.200
you even can be further
away from the action.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:32.440
I like to be closer than
many people because I like

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:33.800
to feel a part of the action.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:35.520
I love to be able
to walk on the set.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:38.360
I love to be able to see
the problems that the boom

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:39.200
operators have.

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:43.360
But yeah, normally, I'm maybe
within 100 feet of the set.

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:46.760
And the sequences
in Pearl Harbor

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.960
where the Arizona
was tipped over,

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:52.280
I was very far away
because that set so huge.

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:56.160
And you're pushing the
technology of the radiomics,

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:56.640
too.

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:58.400
Because I remember Donovan was--

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:02.280
Donovan Dear who's booming
on that particular film.

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:04.960
Really, we were just at
the end of our transmission

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:08.880
path for our radiomics
with where he was.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:15.720
And the beauty of what you do
is you can fix all our mistakes.

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:20.920
On that very thing, I
think all of us, you know,

00:27:20.920 --> 00:27:23.200
whatever, I get through
my hands and goes

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:24.440
to a rerecording mixer.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:27.520
But it's fun dealing
with your tracks

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:29.920
because they're so crisp.

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:33.800
Can you go over
what your process

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:36.080
is when you start a film?

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:41.800
You go and do a
technical scout on where

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:43.760
they're going to shoot.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:45.800
Do you see what
your problems are?

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:47.640
Who do you consult
with when you're first

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:48.920
starting on a film?

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:50.800
To me, that's a
really important thing

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:55.040
to do is to be able to go and
see the practical locations

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:56.280
that you're going to shoot in.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.880
An sometimes, it's a question
of timing and logistics.

00:27:58.880 --> 00:27:59.880
You're in another movie.

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.360
But when I break down
a script, the one

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:05.840
thing I'm looking for is how
many actors are in the scenes,

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:08.280
is there going to
be music involved,

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:10.520
is there going to be
additional manpower

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:11.640
that we're going to need?

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:14.960
For example, with
Black Panther, that

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:20.080
was a film that I wasn't able to
go on the technical scouts on.

00:28:20.080 --> 00:28:22.840
They were shooting at
a place called OFS.

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:25.160
That was doubling for Wakanda.

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:29.960
And unlike Wakanda, we
were dealing with a factory

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:32.960
that where half of it
was a working factory.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:37.200
So you would hear
forklifts, transformers,

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:40.040
and also the local interstate.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:42.400
But from the production's
point of view,

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:44.280
they had this huge
parking lot where

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:48.440
they were able to build
these huge blue screens.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:51.640
And in particular, it
was a huge sequence

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:58.160
with T'Challa and Killmonger
and Forrest Whitaker

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:00.400
where they go to battle.

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:04.520
And at that point,
costumes weren't an issue.

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:07.320
It all had to be
boomed because it

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:13.640
was just bare knuckle
fighting and swords.

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:16.960
What we dealt with
was three cameras.

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:19.000
We were doing three cameras.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.760
And wides and tights.

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:25.200
When you shoot wide and tight,
that can always be a problem.

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:27.120
Because you're trying
to get the microphone

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:30.160
to service the closest camera.

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.800
And fortunately,
we had blue screen,

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:35.200
so we were able to really
push the frame there.

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:37.000
But the one thing that
we were dealing with

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:38.760
was because of the
water-- because we

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:42.400
were shooting at this waterfall,
there was practical effects.

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:46.520
And the cameras all
had special adapters

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:51.400
on the lenses that would deflect
the water if it hit the lens.

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:54.400
The problem is that
they would start it up

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:55.600
during the dialogue.

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.960
And part of the job as
a production mixer, it's

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:01.560
all about negotiation.

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:05.800
You got to negotiate with
the gaffer about the lighting

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:08.080
ballast that's
making a noise on set

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:12.440
or the camera operator
who is shooting

00:30:12.440 --> 00:30:15.880
a wide shot on a tight shot and
we talk to the director about,

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:19.480
is there any chance we can
shoot the wide shots first?

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:23.520
But our job has now
become to be able to adapt

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:25.200
to those circumstances.

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:29.960
And there are some directors
that are pretty adaptable

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:31.480
and they understand
the difficulties

00:30:31.480 --> 00:30:32.280
that you're having.

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.160
And then, there are
other directors that say,

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:35.320
we've got to go.

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:36.160
We don't have time.

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:38.040
I'd love to be able to
accommodate sound here,

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:40.320
but the sun's going down.

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:43.440
This three camera set up we'll
just make my life much easier.

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:46.360
And sometimes, there's not even
time for that conversation.

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:49.960
But that particular
sequence was worked out well

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:51.880
because we talked
to the camera team

00:30:51.880 --> 00:30:58.000
and they turned this
adaptor off so that we were

00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:59.560
able to get the dialogue clean.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:02.480
And at the end of the clip
that our viewers should

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:06.440
be able to see, you'll hear
the high pitched whine come in

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:09.560
after Forrest Whitaker
says his line of dialogue.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:13.680
Let the challenge begin.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:28.560
 Let the challenge begin.

00:31:32.280 --> 00:32:06.240
[SWORDS CLANGING]

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:07.960
You.

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:17.160
So that's about negotiation.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:19.080
But we didn't know that
the camera department

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:21.320
were going to have those
elements on their camera.

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:26.400
So that was problem
solving on the day.

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:30.000
One thing as a production mixer,
it is about problem solving.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.160
You're in real environments.

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:33.240
It's real life.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:35.960
You showed me a
prime example of that

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:38.760
and you asked me to
visit the set once.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:41.920
And I think you were
shooting Daredevil.

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:44.400
You were in downtown
Los Angeles.

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:47.760
And this is a prime example
of them not allowing you

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:52.320
on a scouting to know
what your location is.

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:54.520
Because you invited me up there.

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:57.720
You were up on the roof
and you said to me,

00:32:57.720 --> 00:32:59.560
look what I have to deal with.

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:02.640
There's gravel on the rooftop.

00:33:02.640 --> 00:33:05.000
And these actors were
walking all around,

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.560
which means that anything
that was being recorded

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:09.320
was going to pick up their feet.

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:12.120
But you quickly adapted.

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:14.120
Can you tell me
how you did that?

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.200
It's funny thing, when
I think about Daredevil,

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:19.480
I think about a cricket
that was chirping

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:21.440
during a particular scene.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:24.480
George Flores, who was
my utility, literally

00:33:24.480 --> 00:33:28.800
trying to search out for
this chirp during a--

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:32.440
it was one of the romantic
moments between Ben Affleck

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.200
and Jennifer Garner.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:37.400
But if the camera's
not seeing the floor,

00:33:37.400 --> 00:33:40.560
we would normally
put carpeting down

00:33:40.560 --> 00:33:43.600
to try to muffle any
noises that are going

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:45.520
to obscure the soundtrack.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:49.920
That's just-- I mean, you watch
a movie like Downton Abbey,

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.320
and hard surfaces, hard floors.

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:55.920
I just remember in front
of Downton Abbey there

00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:57.520
was this huge gravel area.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:33:59.680
And I remember seeing a
behind the scenes photograph

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:01.760
of the amount of
carpets that were

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:05.360
put to kind of muffle the
footsteps of the actors

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:06.960
as they move around.

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:10.640
I've worked with a director
Joel Schumacher who

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:13.640
when he saw me putting carpets
down got really irritated

00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:17.160
and said, I hate
the sound of Foley.

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:19.440
I want natural sound effects.

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:22.520
But the natural sound
effects obscure the dialogue.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:27.000
So you're trying to
strike a balance.

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:28.240
There are films that--

00:34:28.240 --> 00:34:31.680
it's funny, when I think about
Foley, I loved good Foley.

00:34:31.680 --> 00:34:35.960
There was Cate Blanchett
in the film Elizabeth.

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:39.920
I just remember the beauty of
those foot steps that are--

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:44.840
some were done on location,
where some done afterwards.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:48.480
And that is, I'm thinking
about all the elements

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.240
that go into the
work that we do.

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:54.440
We try to strip
everything away to give--

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:58.280
to make it as clean as possible
for people in your world

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:00.480
to come in and use
their imagination

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:04.120
to tell a different story.

00:35:04.120 --> 00:35:07.400
For example, Chris Pine
did a walk and talk

00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:09.120
in Wonder Woman 84.

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:10.800
And Chris drags his feet.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.400
And I would have to
say to him, Chris,

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:13.480
can you pick your feet up?

00:35:13.480 --> 00:35:18.080
Because his scuffs
on the concrete

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:19.720
was obscuring the dialogue.

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:22.560
We eventually-- thanks to
the wardrobe department,

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:27.640
we actually resoled his shoes
whenever we made the company

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:28.680
move to the UK.

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:34.160
The fact that we can get the
wardrobe department to work

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:37.080
alongside us to make changes.

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:39.200
I wanted to bring
up some of your work

00:35:39.200 --> 00:35:42.120
that you've done that's quieter.

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:44.080
That's a dialogue sequence.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:46.160
Something like Frost Nixon.

00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:48.760
And I had the pleasure of
working with those tracks.

00:35:48.760 --> 00:35:52.480
And I just want people to
realize that because of the way

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:56.440
you recorded these
things, the sound itself

00:35:56.440 --> 00:36:02.320
becomes a dramatic part of
the emotion of the scene.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:06.520
There was a particular scene
with the last interview

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:08.320
between Frost and Nixon.

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:14.000
And his swallow, his
inhale, his exhale,

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:17.360
were so dramatic
and so clean that it

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:19.040
made my job really easy.

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:20.920
And it sounds pristine.

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:22.400
Is hunt.

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:25.160
Three, don't we have to
handle the Hunt situation?

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:27.080
Four, get the million bucks.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:29.440
It would seem to me that
would be worthwhile.

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:31.120
Five, don't you
agree that you better

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:32.440
get the Hunt thing going?

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:34.360
Six, first, you've
got the Hunt problem.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:35.440
That ought to be handled.

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:37.160
Seven, the money
can be provided.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:39.600
Ehrlichman could provide
the way to deliver it.

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:43.400
Eight, we've no choice
with Hunt, but the $120,000

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:44.440
or whatever it is, right?

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:47.800
Nine, Christ, turn over
any cash we've got.

00:36:47.800 --> 00:36:48.560
And I could go on.

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:51.080
Now, it seems to me that
someone running a coverup

00:36:51.080 --> 00:36:52.880
couldn't have expressed
it more clearly--

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:54.840
Look, let me just stop
you now right there.

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:58.240
Because you're doing something
here, which I am not doing

00:36:58.240 --> 00:36:59.440
and I will not do.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:01.720
Throughout this
entire broadcasts,

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:04.920
you're quoting me out of
context, out of order,

00:37:04.920 --> 00:37:08.880
and I might add, I have
participated in all--

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:15.640
Is it more difficult to shoot
a plain book dialogue scene

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:18.560
than it is an action scene?

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:21.480
It can be.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:24.200
With Frost Nixon,
that was a film

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:26.360
where these two
actors had already

00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:27.720
worked on the stage version.

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:30.800
So they were
incredibly prepared.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.800
And I can only think
of two occasions

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:37.520
where they flubbed
their lines in the film.

00:37:37.520 --> 00:37:38.480
They were so perfect.

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:41.760
Michael Sheen as David
Frost and Frank Langella

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:44.000
as Richard Nixon.

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:47.600
And what I loved about
those guys as well,

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:50.840
Michael Sheen is
a great character.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:51.440
Very funny.

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:55.240
But Frank Langella stayed
in persona as the president.

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:57.680
So when we had to put
a microphone on him,

00:37:57.680 --> 00:37:59.120
we had to call
him Mr. President.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.480
I never saw him out of character
through the whole movie.

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:09.200
And that big final interview
that you talk about,

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:12.280
the silences were
part of the scene--

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:13.760
part of the dramatic moments.

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:18.120
And we were using
close mics and we also

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:20.920
had their period
microphones as well

00:38:20.920 --> 00:38:23.760
because I gave those
to you as a choice

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:26.600
to be able to use if needed.

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:30.760
But I just remember
that it was electric.

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:33.760
And I think David Frost
had actually come to set--

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:38.320
the real David Frost had come
to set during those final days

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:39.080
on the film.

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:41.120
And it's a film
that I'm certainly

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:43.960
very proud of because it is--
it's all about the dialogue.

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:45.920
And Hans Zimmer did the score.

00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:49.840
And there's a
minimalism to his score.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:54.480
And music is such an
important part of films

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:56.720
because it can move you.

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:00.200
And we have no idea how a
film is going to end up.

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:03.400
My job is to record
the performance

00:39:03.400 --> 00:39:07.800
as well as I can to give it
to you where you can clean it

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:11.880
up and assemble it and give
it to the rerecording mixers

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:14.720
and maintain that authenticity.

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:18.480
And for the composers to come
in to punctuate it with the way

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:19.000
they do.

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:20.040
And the Foley artists.

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:25.360
And Midge Costin's
documentary Making Waves,

00:39:25.360 --> 00:39:27.600
you talk about that
circle of talent.

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:29.800
All these different
people that come together

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:32.080
that we don't always
know each other.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:33.880
And I've been
fortunate to know you

00:39:33.880 --> 00:39:35.160
over the course of 20 years.

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:37.680
And I still get
excited when I go

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:41.760
to a dubbing stage and hearing
that original production track

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:46.000
and how it's improved and how
it becomes just a new life

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:50.040
form in itself because
of the talents of others.

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:53.560
Because I would be lost
going into a protools session

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:55.200
and cutting a scene.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:57.200
And you would be lost
coming onto a set

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:00.640
and dealing with the
politics and the machinations

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:02.880
of a production.

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:05.400
It's the collaboration
that goes on between

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:08.400
these different elements of
the soundtrack of the motion

00:40:08.400 --> 00:40:10.480
picture that are so wonderful.

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:13.560
It's even the choices that
are made on a dub stage.

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:17.360
Should we go on to--

00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:19.200
Some questions
from our students.

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:20.440
Our students.

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:29.160
I do have-- maybe we should
clarify the term sound design.

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:32.920
Can we go into that a little bit
so that people can understand?

00:40:32.920 --> 00:40:38.080
It seems to me that sound
design starts from the script.

00:40:38.080 --> 00:40:41.800
And it starts from between
the dialogue, the descriptions

00:40:41.800 --> 00:40:45.000
of what is going to take place
in that particular scene.

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:49.520
And it carries through to
the director's vision of how

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:52.760
he wants the film to sound.

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:56.160
Your contribution is
part of the sound design.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:58.960
My contribution is part
of the sound design.

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:01.800
There are also people
that do sound design

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:09.400
for specific elements of,
say, non-existent creatures.

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:13.000
But I just want to clarify
that sound, in general,

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.480
is so many different levels.

00:41:15.480 --> 00:41:18.360
And maybe you can
touch on that, too,

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:22.320
a little bit before
we go into question.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:24.360
I've been asked
so many times when

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:26.880
people have come
to visit me on set,

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:29.200
do you put the music in now?

00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:31.200
Do you do the sound effects now?

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:35.880
They think that what we do on
set is the finished soundtrack.

00:41:35.880 --> 00:41:39.120
And people have no
idea of just the number

00:41:39.120 --> 00:41:41.080
of tracks and the
weeks and months

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:43.680
that go in to
finish sound track.

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:47.880
And just how a rerecording
mixer sound designer

00:41:47.880 --> 00:41:50.280
looks at a script so
very differently from me.

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:51.800
I mean, I touched
on this earlier.

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:53.720
When I look at a
script, I'm looking

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:56.920
at how many actors am
I going to be working

00:41:56.920 --> 00:41:58.520
with in a particular scene.

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:00.160
Does it mean I'm
going to need more

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:02.360
channels on my mixing console?

00:42:02.360 --> 00:42:05.320
More radiomics?

00:42:05.320 --> 00:42:07.640
When you look at
my notes, you're

00:42:07.640 --> 00:42:09.920
looking at those problem
areas where there may be

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:13.560
noise obscuring a performance.

00:42:13.560 --> 00:42:17.320
The sound designer is
looking for creative moments

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:20.440
that he can find
within the script that

00:42:20.440 --> 00:42:23.200
may not be in the words.

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:26.920
I look at a film like Gravity
that won the Academy Award.

00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:30.240
So much of that
takes place in space

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:32.600
with one single character.

00:42:32.600 --> 00:42:37.280
And it's about the silences and
the breaths and the moments.

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:42.680
And that's also where the
score can come in and just

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:46.800
bring you to a whole
other level emotionally.

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:52.560
Sound is what I find
creates emotion for me.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:56.040
When you turn the
sound off in a film,

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:57.720
it doesn't become
as interesting.

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:00.080
It can be the most
beautifully shot film.

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:04.000
But it's the sound
that brings you in.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:05.200
It sucks me in.

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:07.480
It's also the idea
that, eventually, this

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:08.840
will be in a theater again.

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:10.680
And you never want
to have somebody

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:12.320
say, what did they say?

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:15.320
You want to make sure that
everything is audible.

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:18.200
That nothing pulls you
out of the dramatic

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.800
or the comedic moment, either.

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:22.880
So you want to make sure that--

00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:25.240
our craft is invisible.

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:30.480
Thinking of people that want
to get into this industry.

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:34.760
It is a different world today
because of the internet.

00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:38.600
Because of the ability to
shoot a film on your iPhone.

00:43:38.600 --> 00:43:41.680
And also just-- I
was illustrating

00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:43.120
this little microphone here.

00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:45.160
This can do so much.

00:43:45.160 --> 00:43:49.000
You have one microphone and
this is a little lavaliere

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:52.120
microphone here
and a transmitter.

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:56.400
You can tell a story with
your camera with those--

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:58.000
I mean, I'm still
using those today.

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:02.920
Still relying on a person
holding a microphone just out

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:05.040
of frame to tell a story.

00:44:05.040 --> 00:44:08.280
And the tools are all
there for film students

00:44:08.280 --> 00:44:10.480
to be able to use
to tell their story.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:15.840
I mean, I was looking at some
of the videos on FilmAid.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:20.080
And it was a little
documentary just looking

00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:22.960
at life at the refugee camp.

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:25.920
And it was shot very simply.

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:28.400
But it's all about the
story that's being told.

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:31.720
And sound is part of your story.

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:37.120
And I think it's great
that what FilmAid is doing.

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:39.520
And what this program
is doing to be

00:44:39.520 --> 00:44:43.120
able to bring our knowledge
to a whole new audience

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:45.280
and to inspire as well.

00:44:45.280 --> 00:44:49.120
People that have touched
my life and have allowed

00:44:49.120 --> 00:44:50.960
me to take this journey.

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:54.800
There's a young man
called Willis Abuto

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:57.200
who lives in Nairobi.

00:44:57.200 --> 00:45:00.120
And I got to know Willis.

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:03.720
I think it was when I was doing
the last Transformers films.

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:06.080
I was flying to Namibia
only for two days

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:07.520
and we were looking
for a boom man.

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:09.280
And because of the
power of the internet,

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:11.200
because of the power
of social media,

00:45:11.200 --> 00:45:15.200
I saw that Willis was
a sound mixer working

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:18.360
and in Nairobi and Kenya.

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:20.680
We exchanged conversations.

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:22.360
Unfortunately, he
wasn't able to join me.

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:26.240
But he's somebody that I now
followed in terms of the work

00:45:26.240 --> 00:45:27.160
that he's been doing.

00:45:27.160 --> 00:45:32.840
And he's very busy as a
sound mixer in Nairobi.

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:35.360
And I was just talking
to him last week.

00:45:35.360 --> 00:45:38.160
And we were talking about
microphones and the problems

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:40.720
that I have here,
he has over there,

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:45.320
whether it be wide and tight
shots or noisy locations.

00:45:45.320 --> 00:45:49.040
It's fascinating to see
how you speak the language

00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:51.040
whether you're thousands
of miles apart,

00:45:51.040 --> 00:45:52.880
it's the same language.

00:45:52.880 --> 00:45:54.400
So you're working in sound.

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:59.320
You connected with someone who
you became a mentor to someone.

00:45:59.320 --> 00:46:01.720
By your experiences,
you can help them.

00:46:01.720 --> 00:46:05.200
And it's a way of
paying it forward.

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:11.640
And I find great satisfaction
in finding these diamonds

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:13.480
and finding these
people that are

00:46:13.480 --> 00:46:17.320
interested in doing sound
and carrying on the work

00:46:17.320 --> 00:46:18.560
that we do.

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:21.400
So I think whether it's
FilmAid or whether it's

00:46:21.400 --> 00:46:24.760
your connection to the sound--

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:27.720
the production sound
mixer that you found,

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:30.400
it's all a worthy
journey and it's

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:31.800
giving them the opportunity.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:33.200
So I think that's great.

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:36.400
I actually think we covered
most of the questions that

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:39.720
were asked in advance.

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:46.000
There were some things
that it came up as

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:50.600
to having tough conversations
with various departments.

00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:54.600
This was one of the questions.

00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:56.800
Having a conversation with
the camera department,

00:46:56.800 --> 00:46:58.520
having a conversation
with the director,

00:46:58.520 --> 00:47:00.880
having a conversation
with costumes about

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:03.920
how it relates to sound
and how you navigate that.

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:06.240
Well, you're talking
about costume there.

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:10.480
Ruth Carter was such a
help on Black Panther.

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:13.560
Because when I saw the costume
for Black Panther, I'm like,

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:16.720
how can we get that sound good?

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:18.800
I mean, how are we going
to get that sound at all?

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:21.840
Because it just looks
like a leather suit.

00:47:21.840 --> 00:47:23.960
But it was actually transparent.

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:27.200
There was a spot that we were
able to put in the perfect spot

00:47:27.200 --> 00:47:28.520
on Chadwick's chest.

00:47:28.520 --> 00:47:31.560
And it was-- acoustically,
it worked out great.

00:47:31.560 --> 00:47:35.760
But Ruth and I started
talking very early on.

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:40.280
And she was very aware
of what we needed to do.

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:44.400
And that's what's great about
being able to have that time

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:47.360
to work with those
departments to figure out

00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:50.040
where the problems can be.

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:55.680
On this last show that I did, we
were dealing with noisy lights.

00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:59.920
And that was a thorn in my side
through the course of the film.

00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:02.480
And 20 years ago,
I think I would

00:48:02.480 --> 00:48:04.960
have been just so distressed.

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:08.840
But now, I know there are tools
that can remove that background

00:48:08.840 --> 00:48:09.840
noise.

00:48:09.840 --> 00:48:11.200
It doesn't make
me happy that I'm

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:13.280
having to deal with
it on the time,

00:48:13.280 --> 00:48:17.080
but at least they're there.

00:48:17.080 --> 00:48:19.160
Technology helps us sometimes.

00:48:19.160 --> 00:48:22.640
The technology changes
are coming along

00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:26.520
and I think will help us in
all kind of areas like that.

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:29.480
If I could just mention one
thing about learning always

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:30.600
being educated.

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:32.880
Because I'm still learning.

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:36.600
There's a film that the Academy
screened last year that I

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:39.080
got to see it before COVID.

00:48:39.080 --> 00:48:42.080
And it was from 1936 and it
was a black and white film

00:48:42.080 --> 00:48:44.280
and it won the Academy
Award for sound.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:47.240
And it was with Mary
Astor, William--

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:50.040
Walter Houston, and it was
directed by William Weiler.

00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:51.880
It was all shot on stage.

00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:54.240
But there was a sequence
that takes place in the boat.

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:58.600
And they built the front
section of a boat on stage.

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:00.320
And I could see the hair moving.

00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:05.200
Now, when it comes to her moving
and their clothes ruffling,

00:49:05.200 --> 00:49:07.200
you're talking
about Pearl Harbor.

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:08.640
You're talking about noise.

00:49:08.640 --> 00:49:12.880
On Wonder Woman, we had to
deal with wind effects in that.

00:49:12.880 --> 00:49:16.800
And it is so difficult. So I'm
watching this film from 1936

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:21.200
going, how are they doing that?

00:49:21.200 --> 00:49:24.280
How on Earth are they
getting such grit?

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:27.840
Because that does not sound like
it's being replaced afterwards.

00:49:27.840 --> 00:49:32.680
So and even last week, I was
looking at a new microphone

00:49:32.680 --> 00:49:34.240
that I haven't used before.

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:36.600
And I was like, oh,
this is interesting.

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:38.840
This is something I
could definitely use.

00:49:38.840 --> 00:49:42.320
So you're always learning.

00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:43.560
Absolutely.

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:45.800
Peter, I could talk
to you for hours.

00:49:45.800 --> 00:49:49.840
And unfortunately, we
are running out of time.

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:51.120
I want to thank you.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:54.440
And I hope that everybody
listening to this

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:57.400
gained a little bit of
knowledge and a little bit

00:49:57.400 --> 00:49:58.280
of understanding.

00:49:58.280 --> 00:50:01.760
And we could sort of
pull back the curtain

00:50:01.760 --> 00:50:04.200
on what we do in sound.

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:06.320
So thank you very much.

00:50:06.320 --> 00:50:07.560
Thank you, Teri.

